A palette manager notifies many brush widgets of colour updates. The manager keeps a list of registered listeners and calls them in a loop:

```java
public void publish(Colour colour) {
    for (BrushListener listener : listeners) {
        listener.onColourUpdate(colour);
    }
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A configuration class in a painting application is written like this and is read from every brush handler:

```java
public class PaletteConfig {
    private static PaletteConfig instance;
    private PaletteConfig() { }
    public static PaletteConfig getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new PaletteConfig();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Which criticisms of this code are justified?

A colour‑blending pipeline fixes the order of the steps and lets subclasses vary two of them:

```java
public abstract class BlendPipeline {
    public final BlendResult blend() {
        Data data = load();
        Data shaped = transform(data);
        return write(render(shaped));
    }
    protected Data load() { return Data.fromDefaultSource(); }
    protected abstract Data transform(Data data);
    protected abstract String render(Data data);
}
```

Which statements about this design are correct?

A painting tool sorts colour palettes with interchangeable algorithms:

```java
public interface SortStrategy { void performSort(List<Colour> palette); }
public class HueSort implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class SaturationSort  implements SortStrategy { ... }
public class Context { private List<Colour> palette; private SortStrategy sortAlgorithm; ... }
public class Policy  { private Context context; public void configure() { ... } }
```

Using the class names from this listing: the class that holds the palette and a reference to the currently selected algorithm is [-spot 1]. The type that both `HueSort` and `SaturationSort` implement is [-spot 2]. The class whose `configure()` method decides which algorithm to select is [-spot 3].

A reviewer rejects the following order notification service:

```java
public class OrderService {
    private final SmtpMailer mailer = new SmtpMailer("mail.internal", 25);
    public void placeOrder(Order order) { /* ... */ mailer.send(order.customerEmail(), "..."); }
}
```

The reviewer's first objection is that `OrderService` names a concrete low‑level class instead of an abstraction such as a `Notifier` interface; the principle being violated is abbreviated [-spot 1] (three letters). The reviewer's fix is to stop constructing the collaborator inside the class and supply it from outside instead — the general two‑word name for that technique is [-spot 2].
